## Functions That Give You Ownership

Functions can create values and return them. Ownership transfers from the function to the caller:

```xiom
fn create_greeting(name: Str) -> Str {
  let greeting = "Hello, " + name + "!";
  return greeting;  // Ownership moves to caller
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let msg = create_greeting("XIOM");  // We now own the string
  io.println(msg);
  return msg.len() as Int;
}
```

### Factory Functions

```xiom
fn build_vec() -> Vec[Int] {
  let v = Vec[Int].new();
  v.push(1);
  v.push(2);
  v.push(3);
  return v;  // Returns ownership to caller
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let my_vec = build_vec();
  io.println(my_vec.len());  // 3
  return my_vec.len() as Int;
}
```

### Ownership Chains

```xiom
fn make_vec() -> Vec[Int] { /* ... */ return v; }
fn double_vec(v: Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] { /* ... */ return v; }

fn main() -> Int {
  let v = double_vec(make_vec());  // make_vec → double_vec → main
  return v.len() as Int;
}
```

### Return Values Are Moves

When you return a local variable, its ownership moves to the caller. The compiler guarantees the value stays alive until it reaches its new owner.

